+# Protocol evolution: two builds, one cluster
+
+A rolling upgrade is the third time this project holds two truths at once: joint consensus (`5.5`) held
+two memberships, certificate rotation (`5.9`) held two certificates, and this holds two **builds**. The
+rule is the same arithmetic in a different unit — every node has to be able to understand every other
+node, at every instant of the change — and two numbers make it checkable.
+
+## The two numbers
+
+- **major** is the wire format. Two builds with different majors cannot talk at all, so a rolling
+  upgrade may never have both in a running cluster. A major bump is a *stop-the-world* change, and the
+  plan refuses to pretend otherwise.
+- **minor** is compatible inside a major, and the compatibility is **asymmetric**: a build parses every
+  minor up to its own and no further. The newer build is therefore the one that has to hold back,
+  because it is the one that could send a frame the older cannot read.
+
+That asymmetry is the whole constraint of a rolling upgrade. A node upgraded to `1.2` speaks `1.1` on
+the wire until the last node is upgraded; only then does the cluster start speaking `1.2`.
+
+## The plan
+
+```text
+order:   n1        n2        n3
+phase:   compat    compat    native
+speaks:  1.1       1.1       1.2
+```
+
+The plan is an order plus the phases it produces, and an operator reads the phases off a deploy log:
+"n1 and n2 are new builds running in compatibility mode, n3 is where the cluster moves". A plan that
+claimed to cross a major is refused, and so is one that upgrades nobody.
+
+## The handshake
+
+The handshake carries the version, for one reason: the moment a node sees a version it cannot parse,
+the right answer is a refusal that **names both versions** — an operator reading it has to know which
+build is the odd one out. A frame that gets misread is a cluster that behaves strangely for reasons
+nobody can see; a refusal with two version numbers in it is a five-minute fix.
+
+## What this buys
+
+The same thing every "two truths" step bought: a change that can be *planned* rather than hoped for.
+The version rule is a sentence ("the newer build holds back"), the plan is a value, and the check runs
+before the deploy.
